<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Peace of Prague (1866)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1866">1866</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-prussian-war">Austro-Prussian War</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Peace of Prague, signed on 23 August 1866, ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-prussian-war">Austro-Prussian War</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> accepted the dissolution of the German Confederation and its own exclusion from German affairs, leaving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> free to annex <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a>, Hesse-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kassel">Kassel</a>, Nassau, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schleswig-holstein">Schleswig-Holstein</a> and to form the North German Confederation. Austria also confirmed the cession of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venetia">Venetia</a>, which passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/peace-of-prague-1866.jpg" alt="Peace of Prague (1866)" width="320" height="176" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bilderrevolution0270.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
